## Why This Is Asked

To assess the ability to design policy-aware, scalable agent orchestration that handles multi-tenant data isolation and observability.

## Key Concepts

- LangChain agent orchestration with AutoGen planning
- Private APIs and data catalog integration
- Policy enforcement and tenant isolation
- End-to-end traceability, auditing, and failure handling
- Resilience patterns: retries, backoff, circuit breakers

## Code Example

```javascript
async function plan(request) {
  const { userId, tenantId, dataScope } = request;

  const policyResult = await policyEngine.check({ tenantId, dataScope });
  if (!policyResult.allowed) {
    throw new Error('Access denied: policy violation');
  }

  const catalogResult = await dataCatalog.query({ tenantId, dataScope });
  const apiResult = await privateApi.fetch({ tenantId, dataScope });

  await auditLogger.log({
    tenantId,
    userId,
    steps: [policyResult, catalogResult, apiResult],
    traceId: uuid()
  });

  return { data: apiResult, traceId: traceId };
}
```

This flow ensures tenant-scoped policies are enforced at entry, each component call is traced, and failures are handled with compensating transactions or graceful degradation.

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical depth in building AI-driven data pipelines with LangChain, AutoGen, and tool orchestration. It evaluates planning discipline, error handling strategies, and observability in a realistic analytics context.

## Key Concepts

- LangChain agent orchestration and planning
- Tool invocation patterns with idempotent design
- Caching strategies, retry/backoff mechanisms, and failure-mode handling
- Observability, tracing, and testing best practices

## Code Example

```javascript
import { Chain, Tool, Planner } from 'langchain';
import { AgentExecutor } from 'langchain/agents';
import Redis from 'ioredis';

const redis = new Redis(process.env.REDIS_URL);

const apiTool = new Tool({
  name: 'fetch_analytics',
  description: 'Fetches user analytics from the private REST API. Input: JSON with query parameters.',
  func: async (input, runManager) => {
    const response = await fetchWithRetry(input);
    return JSON.stringify(response);
  }
});

async function fetchWithRetry(input, maxRetries = 3) {
  const baseDelay = 1000;
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt < maxRetries; attempt++) {
    try {
      const response = await fetch('/api/analytics', {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
        body: input
      });
      if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);
      return await response.json();
    } catch (err) {
      if (attempt === maxRetries - 1 || !isRetriable(err)) throw err;
      await sleep(baseDelay * Math.pow(2, attempt));
    }
  }
}

const cacheMiddleware = async (req, next) => {
  const cacheKey = `analytics:${JSON.stringify(req)}`;
  const cached = await redis.get(cacheKey);
  if (cached) return JSON.parse(cached);

  const result = await next();
  await redis.setex(cacheKey, 3600, JSON.stringify(result));
  return result;
};
```

## Why This Is Asked
Streaming generation with chunked inputs is common in real-time chat; understanding and controlling cross-chunk attention drift is essential for reliability, especially in multilingual contexts.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming/incremental generation and chunk boundaries
- Attention drift across chunks and languages
- Boundary-aware gating vs retraining
- Lightweight diagnostic pipelines and drift metrics (KL/JS)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal example: compute per-head attention mass on current chunk
function headMass(attn, currentIdx, prevCount) {
  // attn: [heads][tokens][tokens]
  const heads = attn.length;
  const currentMass = [];
  for (let h = 0; h < heads; h++) {
    let sumAll = 0;
    let sumCurrent = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < attn[h].length; i++) {
      for (let j = 0; j < attn[h][i].length; j++) {
        sumAll += attn[h][i][j];
        if (i >= currentIdx - prevCount && i < currentIdx) {
          sumCurrent += attn[h][i][j];
        }
      }
    }
    currentMass.push(sumCurrent / (sumAll || 1));
  }
  return currentMass;
}
```

